WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.148 --> 00:00:02.537
Hi, I'm Kayla Brandt.

2
00:00:02.537 --> 00:00:07.000
I've run the IT help desk at Kestrel Falls
Community Hospital for six years.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.141
Laura Hemmingsen asked me what the
picture looks like from my desk,

4
00:00:10.141 --> 00:00:12.792
so let me tell you.

5
00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:13.392
Honestly?

6
00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:16.225
My team is already behind on tickets,
and that's on a normal week.

7
00:00:16.225 --> 00:00:20.397
We support everybody, about nineteen
hundred people across the hospital,

8
00:00:20.397 --> 00:00:22.720
the clinics, and the ambulance service.

9
00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:25.246
Password resets, printers, badge readers,

10
00:00:25.246 --> 00:00:28.977
"my screen froze," new hires who
don't have access yet.

11
00:00:28.977 --> 00:00:30.590
On a good day we keep up.

12
00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:33.107
On a bad day the queue grows
and people wait.

13
00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:35.321
At go-live, the phones will light up.

14
00:00:35.321 --> 00:00:38.275
Everyone will call at once:
password problems, printers,

15
00:00:38.275 --> 00:00:43.362
"where did my patient list go." My fear is a
flood of calls we can't answer,

16
00:00:43.362 --> 00:00:46.726
and frustrated staff who decide the new system
is broken when really they just need

17
00:00:46.726 --> 00:00:48.705
help finding a button.

18
00:00:48.705 --> 00:00:53.152
Once a unit decides the system is broken, it's
really hard to change their minds,

19
00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:53.752
even after things settle down.

20
00:00:53.152 --> 00:00:58.893
In my experience, a lot of go-live calls
are not really technical.

21
00:00:58.893 --> 00:01:03.035
"How do I find this." "Where did that report
go." "I can't print a label." Those

22
00:01:03.035 --> 00:01:07.682
are training and workflow questions, and they
clog the line for the real problems,

23
00:01:07.682 --> 00:01:10.320
like an interface that stopped
sending lab results.

24
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:15.288
If my team spends the morning on "where's the
button," the lab problem sits in the

25
00:01:15.288 --> 00:01:16.517
queue.

26
00:01:16.517 --> 00:01:17.631
So what do I need?

27
00:01:17.631 --> 00:01:18.335
Two things.

28
00:01:18.335 --> 00:01:23.144
First, super users: one or two trained people on
every unit and every shift who can

29
00:01:23.144 --> 00:01:27.203
answer the easy questions right there, so
they never become tickets.

30
00:01:27.203 --> 00:01:29.946
Second, extra hands at go-live.

31
00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:32.219
Vendor staff, temporary staff, whoever,

32
00:01:32.219 --> 00:01:36.373
standing on the floors for the first
two weeks, around the clock.

33
00:01:36.373 --> 00:01:38.815
People call it at-the-elbow support.

34
00:01:38.815 --> 00:01:41.982
A person at your elbow fixes your
problem in thirty seconds.

35
00:01:41.982 --> 00:01:42.582
A ticket takes an hour.

36
00:01:41.982 --> 00:01:46.216
The super users connect to my team
through a command center.

37
00:01:46.216 --> 00:01:47.900
One room, open twenty-four hours,

38
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.274
where super users and floor support can
call or message us directly.

39
00:01:52.274 --> 00:01:54.899
We sort problems by how serious they are.

40
00:01:54.899 --> 00:01:56.769
Anything that touches patient
safety jumps the line.

41
00:01:56.769 --> 00:02:00.136
And we keep a running list
of known issues,

42
00:02:00.136 --> 00:02:02.428
so when fifty people ask the
same question,

43
00:02:02.428 --> 00:02:04.521
the floor support already has the answer.

44
00:02:04.521 --> 00:02:07.572
I also need training to happen
before go-live, not during.

45
00:02:07.572 --> 00:02:13.438
Every person who walks in on day one without a
working login is a call to my desk.

46
00:02:13.438 --> 00:02:14.744
So everybody tests their
login a week ahead.

47
00:02:14.744 --> 00:02:17.581
We fix access problems then,
while we have time.

48
00:02:17.581 --> 00:02:21.969
And my own team needs training first,
deeper than everybody else,

49
00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:26.769
so we're not learning the system at the
same time we're supporting it.

50
00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:31.584
And please, whatever you do, don't schedule
go-live over a holiday weekend.

51
00:02:31.584 --> 00:02:33.718
We tried that once with a smaller system.

52
00:02:33.718 --> 00:02:34.317
Half my team was on vacation.

53
00:02:33.718 --> 00:02:37.890
Pick a date when we can have
everybody here,

54
00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:40.368
and block vacations for the
two weeks after.

55
00:02:40.368 --> 00:02:42.471
How will we know if support is working?

56
00:02:42.471 --> 00:02:43.448
We'll track it.

57
00:02:43.448 --> 00:02:47.661
Calls per day, how long people wait, how
many tickets are still open,

58
00:02:47.661 --> 00:02:49.548
and the top ten problems.

59
00:02:49.548 --> 00:02:53.790
If the same problem keeps showing up, that's a
training gap or a build problem,

60
00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:58.734
and we fix it at the source instead of
answering it a hundred times.

61
00:02:58.734 --> 00:02:59.334
How many super users?

62
00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:03.733
Enough that every unit has someone on every
shift, including nights and weekends.

63
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:07.438
And the clinics and the ambulance bases
too, not just the hospital.

64
00:03:07.438 --> 00:03:10.707
They need to be people their coworkers
already go to for help,

65
00:03:10.707 --> 00:03:12.532
not just whoever volunteers.

66
00:03:12.532 --> 00:03:14.413
And they need real time for it.

67
00:03:14.413 --> 00:03:18.725
If a super user is also carrying a full
patient load, nobody gets helped.

68
00:03:18.725 --> 00:03:23.723
What I want the rest of the project team to
understand about my group is that we're

69
00:03:23.723 --> 00:03:25.458
the face of this project on day one.

70
00:03:25.458 --> 00:03:27.996
People won't call the vendor.

71
00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:28.596
They'll call us.

72
00:03:27.996 --> 00:03:31.329
If we're ready, the whole
thing feels smoother.

73
00:03:31.329 --> 00:03:33.800
If we're not, everybody blames the system.

